Yoga Vāsiṣṭha – Brahma Gītā
Sarga 186 (Part 14)
When the Mind Falls Silent
Sri Vasiṣṭha now gathers the entire teaching into a single, luminous vision.
Pure Consciousness is never bound.
Yet within that limitless Awareness arise the imagined notions of mind, intellect, individuality, and body. Once these are accepted as "I," the magnificent panorama of creation unfolds, with all its joys, sorrows, beginnings, and endings.
But what happens when these mental constructions are no longer taken to be real?
Sri Vasiṣṭha answers with extraordinary simplicity.
Nothing new is attained.
Rather, what always was becomes evident—
the unborn, causeless, non-dual Brahman.
This is the vision of the sage.
To illustrate this, he points to our own experience.
Within one night, the same sleep contains both dream and deep sleep.
Dream presents a world filled with light, activity, and multiplicity.
Deep sleep presents no world at all.
Yet both arise within one underlying reality.
Likewise, creation and dissolution are only alternating appearances within the limitless Brahman.
They do not alter the Reality in which they appear.
The purpose of Vedānta is not to deny the appearance.
It is to awaken us to the changeless Presence that remains untouched whether the universe appears or disappears.
The mind speaks of creation.
Silence reveals Brahman.
Hari Om Tat Sat
